Integer coefficients for the element routines in this addon.


Base functions (shape functions)
--------------------------------

See file addons/README_basefunctions


Numerical integration
---------------------

See file addons/README_Gauss


Procedure pointers
------------------

Summary of assignment of user functions/subroutines to procedure pointers:

  coefficients%vfunc1(1)%p => u ! vector function for velocity u.
  coefficients%vfunc1(2)%p => u_m ! vector function for velocity u_m.

NOTES:

 1) The user functions/subroutines only need to be assigned if they are
    actually being used. If a required assignment is forgotten an error message
    with be given.
 2) The user is free to choose the functions/subroutines for assignment as
    long as the interface is correct.


List of the coefficients
------------------------

1   interpolation of the position of the interface
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral interpolation also the polynomial order p
    needs to be set with coefficients%i(2).

2   Polynomial order p of the spectral/high-order interpolation for the
    position 

3   Numerical integration rule for the internal domain integrals of elements.

4   nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    internal domain integrals of elements:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(3) to the complete element domain.
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate 
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by 
        coefficients%i(3) to each subdomain.

5   Type of integration (inttype):
      0: standard Gauss-Legendre (analytical expressions)
      1: Gauss-Legendre-Lobatto
      2: mapped Gauss (quad->triangle).
      3: standard Gauss-Legendre (numerical tables)

6   The vector u (=velocity) is determined as follows:
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(1:3)
    1: given by a vector function, vfuncnr=coefficient%i(7)
       NOTE: vector function must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc1(1)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(1)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)

7   vfuncnr for the vector u (=velocity)

8   The vector u_m (=arbitrary velocity field) is determined as follows:
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(4:6)
    1: given by a vector function, vfuncnr=coefficient%i(9)
       NOTE: vector function must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc1(2)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(2)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(2)

9   vfuncnr for the vector u_m (=arbitrary velocity field) 

10  If = 1, include the tangential vector u_t. Note, that a scalar field needs
    to be available from oldvectors%s(1)%p using problem oldvectors%p(1)%p.

11  Time integration scheme (timeint):
    1: first-order, implicit Euler
    2: second-order, implicit Gear

12  How to build matrix and vector:
    0: matrix is for one coordinate direction only; vector is build as a 
       multiple right-hand side for each coordinate direction separately.
    1: both matrix are built for all coordinate directions at once, assuming
       a vector of size ndim in each node. Note, that the system needs to be
       built with order='DN', which is not the default if no physical
       quantities have been defined.

13  coordinate system for 2D elements:
    0: Cartesian planar
    1: Axisymmetrical
    Note that for axisymmetrical coordinates we have (z,r), i.e. the first
    coordinate is the axial coordinate z and the second coordinate is the
    radial coordinate r.

14  Interpolation of the velocity on the interface for coefficients%i(6)=2:
      0: interpolation is the same as for the position (coefficients%i(1))
    >=1: independent interpolation for the velocity.
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral, also the polynomial order p needs to be 
          set with coefficients%i(15).

15  Polynomial order p of the spectral interpolation for the velocity

16  Shape of the element:
    0 Use the interpolation of the position (coefficients%i(1))
    1 Use the interpolation of the velocity (coefficients%i(14))

17  Specifies how the integration rules must be set:
    0: default, i.e. direcly with the value in coefficients.
    1: by specifying the minimal polynomial order that the rule must be able
       to integrate exactly.
    Note, that the value of coefficients%i(17) is overruled by setting
    SET_GAUSS_BY_ORDER = .true. in the module limits_m.

18--100 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

